<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1789">1789</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Declaration of the Rights of Man</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen was adopted by the French National Constituent Assembly on 26 August 1789. Drawing on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a> ideas, it declared that men are born and remain free and equal in rights, that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> lies with the nation, and that law is the expression of the general will. It became a founding text of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a> and of modern human rights.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/declaration-of-the-rights-of-man-and-of-the-citizen.jpg" alt="Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen" width="320" height="405" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Declaration_of_the_Rights_of_Man_and_of_the_Citizen_in_1789.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The ideas of the Declaration came largely from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a>: individualism, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-rousseau">Jean-Jacques Rousseau</a>'s social contract and the Baron de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montesquieu">Montesquieu</a>'s separation of powers. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-declaration-of-independence">United States Declaration of Independence</a> of 1776 was an influence too. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marquis-de-lafayette">Marquis de Lafayette</a> prepared the principal early drafts with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-jefferson">Thomas Jefferson</a>, then American diplomat in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Presented to the legislative committee on 11 July 1789, his draft was rejected by pragmatists such as Jean Joseph Mounier, who feared raising expectations that could not be met. The final text owes most to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emmanuel-joseph-sieyes">Emmanuel Joseph Sieyès</a>, with Honoré Mirabeau a central figure in editing it, and it was published on 26 August 1789 as a statement of principle.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>A preamble calls the rights natural, unalienable and sacred, and Article II names them as liberty, property, security and resistance to oppression. Article I declares that men are born and remain free and equal in rights. Article III places <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> in the Nation, in contrast to the divine right of kings, and Article VI calls law the expression of the general will, with all citizens equally admissible to public office according to their capacity.</p><p>Other articles forbid arbitrary arrest, require that penalties be established by law before the offence, presume innocence, protect opinions, including religious ones, as long as they do not trouble public order, and declare the free communication of thoughts one of the most precious rights. Taxes are to be consented to by citizens or their representatives.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The rights were granted only to men, and the 1791 Constitution distinguished active citizens, French men of at least 25 who paid taxes equal to three days' work and were not servants, from passive citizens. About 4.3 million of some 29 million people held the vote. In September 1791 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olympe-de-gouges">Olympe de Gouges</a> answered with her Declaration of the Rights of Woman and of the Female Citizen. The Declaration did not abolish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in the French colonies, but it inspired the uprisings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a> that became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haitian-revolution">Haitian Revolution</a>.</p><p>It was attached as a preamble to the Constitution of 1791 and is included at the start of the constitutions of the Fourth Republic (1946) and Fifth Republic (1958), where it is valid as constitutional law. In 2003 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> added the first printed version to its Memory of the World International Register.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…the Convention drafted the Constitution of 1793, ratified by popular vote in early August, but under the Committee of Public Safety, seen as an emergency government, the rights it guaranteed, and those of the <b>Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen</b> of 1789, were suspended.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-first-republic">French First Republic · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The third estate proclaimed itself the National Assembly in June, the fall of the Bastille on 14 July protected it from royal troops, and in August it abolished feudal privileges and issued the <b>Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Then the <b>Declaration of the Rights of Man</b> of 26 August 1789 reached the island.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In France the National Assembly adopted the <b>Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen</b> in August 1789, weeks after the Storming of the Bastille, and in Poland-Lithuania reformers passed the Constitution of 3 May 1791.</blockquote><a href="/movement/enlightenment">Enlightenment · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>Historians differ over where the programme came from: Butrus Abu-Manneh finds no Western influence in its founding edict, while Stanford Jay Shaw sees the direct influence of the French <b>Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen</b> of 1789.</blockquote><a href="/movement/tanzimat">Tanzimat · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>In Germany Justus Möser and Friedrich von Gentz criticised the <b>Declaration of the Rights of Man</b>.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/conservatism">Conservatism · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
